==============================================================================
NOT EXECUTED — 9 words, crunch-only. Reasons below; these are
COVERAGE HOLES IN THIS PROBE, not evidence of support.
==============================================================================
  pad_win_switch NEEDS-WINDOW:switch SUBJECT:PAD FORM:switch -1 while the pen switch is held, 0 released -- both machines, windowed, 2026-09-27
  stick_rig NOT RUN -- NEEDS-RIG: no rigfw board answered `id` on /dev/cu.usbmodem* (tools/rigfw). NEEDS-RIG:upright SUBJECT:STICK FORM:joystick-port 2 = UP-RIGHT, off port A's direction LINES (the PSG), with STICK(0) -- the cursor keys, nothing held -- still 0 beside it. A direction CODE, not a flag: a stub or a keyboard read cannot produce `2 0`
  stick_rig_b NOT RUN -- NEEDS-RIG: no rigfw board answered `id` on /dev/cu.usbmodem* (tools/rigfw). NEEDS-RIG:downleft SUBJECT:STICK 6 = DOWN-LEFT: the other diagonal, two different lines, so `stick_rig`'s 2 is a decode and not a constant
  strig_rig NOT RUN -- NEEDS-RIG: no rigfw board answered `id` on /dev/cu.usbmodem* (tools/rigfw). NEEDS-RIG:trig1 SUBJECT:STRIG FORM:joystick-trigger -1: port A's trigger A is down, and beside it STRIG(3) (port A's trigger B) and STRIG(0) (the space bar) stay 0
  strig_rig_b NOT RUN -- NEEDS-RIG: no rigfw board answered `id` on /dev/cu.usbmodem* (tools/rigfw). NEEDS-RIG:trig2 SUBJECT:STRIG -1 0: trigger B alone reads through n=3, and n=1 stays 0 -- the index picks the BUTTON, not just the port
